-A technique to fill voxels that are partially interior, addressing the issue where thin geometry misses voxel centers and is annihilated by XOR.
+Conservative Solid Voxelization is a technique to fill voxels that are partially interior, addressing the issue where thin geometry misses voxel centers and is annihilated by XOR blending. It builds on [Single-pass GPU Solid Voxelization](3323-single-pass-gpu-solid-voxelization.md) by adding a conservative boundary pass to ensure no surface-touching voxels are lost, resulting in a conservative solid voxelization.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
 **Time and Memory Complexity:**
-*   **Time:** Single geometry pass. Performance depends on fill rate and geometry complexity.
-*   **Memory:** O(W * H) for the slicemap texture, where W, H are the XY dimensions of the voxel grid. The Z dimension is packed into the bits of the texture.
+- Time: $O(N)$ where $N$ is the number of fragments, performed in a single geometry pass [S1, unofficial].
+- Memory: $O(W \times H \times L)$ where $W, H$ are texture dimensions and $L$ is the number of layers (bits per texel). Typically 32 layers per pass on DX9, up to 1024 on DX10 with MRTs [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
 
 **Measured Performance:**
-*   **DX10 (G80):**
-    *   Density computation timings: 0.20 ms at $64^3$, 0.6 ms at $128^3$, 2.9 ms at $256^3$, 20.9 ms at $512^3$ [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Speedup over CPU-based method [5]: >300x for complex models; ~20x for a simple 12-triangle cube (worst-case due to low geometric complexity and high fill-rate) [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Theoretical maximum speedup is 1024x due to multi-layer extraction per pass [S2, unofficial].
-*   **DX9 (G6):**
-    *   Density computation timings: 0.28 ms at $256^3$, 0.9 ms at $512^3$, 6.5 ms at $1024^3$ [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Speedup over [5]: ~23x for models up to 12,500 triangles [S1, unofficial].
-    *   Without MRT support, 32 layers are extracted per pass. Performance is ~2x the cost of 4 MRTs but still yields significant speedup [S1, unofficial].
+- **DX10 (G80):**
+    - Density computation timings: 0.20 ms at $64^3$, 0.6 ms at $128^3$, 2.9 ms at $256^3$, 20.9 ms at $512^3$ [S1, unofficial].
+    - Speedup over [5]: >300x for complex models; ~20x for a simple 12-triangle cube (worst-case due to low geometry/high fill-rate) [S1, unofficial].
+- **DX9 (G6800):**
+    - Density computation timings: 0.28 ms at $256^3$, 0.9 ms at $512^3$, 6.5 ms at $1024^3$ [S1, unofficial].
+    - Speedup over [5]: ~23x for models up to 12,500 triangles [S1, unofficial].
+    - Without MRT support, 32 layers are extracted per pass, costing ~2x more than 4 MRTs but still achieving 23x speedup over [5] [S1, unofficial].
 
 **Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Single pass; handles arbitrary fragment density per voxel accurately; does not require manifold meshes or front/back face separation; handles grazing angles automatically [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Only works for watertight models; intersecting models result in symmetric difference (hollow) rather than union; standard rasterization framerate drops quickly for high-polygon models and high voxel resolutions [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
-*   **Hardware Specifics:** DX9 implementation treats all four color components equally, reflecting vector capacities of older processors [S1, unofficial]. Using 4 MRTs imposes more than the expected 33% supplementary workload compared to 3 MRTs, likely due to hardware sweet spots (e.g., G6) [S1, unofficial].
+- **Strengths:** Single pass; handles arbitrary fragment density per voxel; no front/back face separation required; works on non-manifold watertight meshes; fast density estimation via box-filtering [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Requires watertight input; intersecting models produce symmetric difference (hollow) rather than union; standard rasterization causes Z-axis shift; performance drops for high-polygon models and high resolutions due to fill-rate limits [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-**Origin:**
-The method was introduced by Elmar Eisemann and Xavier Decoret in their paper "Single-Pass GPU Solid Voxelization for Real-Time Applications" [S2, unofficial]. It builds upon previous work on GPU boundary voxelization by Dong et al. [8] and Eisemann & Decoret [10] [S2, unofficial].
-
-**History and Development:**
-Prior methods like depth peeling required multiple passes. The stencil buffer approach [5] was limited to one voxel layer per rendering pass. This method leverages the bitwise capabilities of modern GPUs (DX10/DX9) to pack up to 1024 binary layers into a single pass using XOR accumulation [S2, unofficial]. It was demonstrated in applications such as translucency effects, CSG operations, particle simulation interaction, and morphological operations [S2, unofficial].
+Originated by Elmar Eisemann and Xavier Decoret in their paper "Single-Pass GPU Solid Voxelization for Real-Time Applications" [S2, unofficial]. It was developed to address the limitations of previous methods like Dong et al. [8] (which failed with multiple fragments per voxel) and Eisemann & Decoret [10] (which required front/back face separation) [S2, unofficial]. It enables real-time solid voxelization for applications like fluid simulation interaction and CSG operations [S2, unofficial].
 
